451:2907. The two out-of-scope repairs
460:- Scope modeling now follows the analyzer and the runtime together: an `if`/`unless` branch body is its own scope, but a `set` inside it writes through to an enclosing binding (or the program scope when the name is new), so it survives `end` while a function, object or import declared there does not. Each `test` body is scoped on its own because the harness runs it in an isolated VM; the previous attempt modeled both as shared, so a name could leak from one test to the next, and the restored isolation fixture pins that distinction. A `constant` always binds in the program scope (the test body's scope inside a `test`), and the catch scope closes when `finally` starts.
461:- A function and a variable of the same spelling share one slot in the runtime, so a later `set`, `constant` or `to` replaces a binding of another kind. Navigation now models that declaration order: the later declaration owns the following uses, while the earlier declaration keeps its own uses. An inner `set` over an outer function creates a local variable rather than writing through the function. No test from `main` was deleted or weakened.
468:- Completion enumerates a program-declared module (and an `import` of it) because its `module … end` body is in the source text the navigation functions receive. A module loaded from a separate file has no declarations in that text, so these pure-source functions cannot enumerate its members; `Vm::module_member_values` can do so once the runtime has loaded the file. Reading module files here would make completion depend on the working directory rather than only on the source, so it is recorded as a follow-up instead.
